#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-citation-check — report every record citation in a consuming project
# that the storeless citation grammar cannot resolve.
#
# Usage:
#   fusion-citation-check [--undecidable]
#
# Output on stdout, one KEY=value per line, then one row per violation:
#
#   anchor=workbench-root
#   root=.
#   files=312
#   edited-files=94
#   declared-patterns=2
#   declared-files=45            # or `unavailable`, see below
#   tokens=1904
#   judged=1211
#   resolved=1180
#   dangling=3
#   store-prefixed=28
#   edited-violations=2
#   unedited-violations=29
#   unrewritable-violations=11
#   undecidable=402
#   exempt=291
#   verdict=violations            # or clean
#     260828-0900_*_….md:14  '260811-1534_*_….md'  store-prefixed  edited  rewritable  …
#
# ONLY A ROW IN A FILE SOMEBODY STILL EDITS MOVES `verdict=` (2026-09-01,
# decision 260830-2225_*_should-an-archived-violation-move-the-checkers-verdict-line.md,
# option 3). Every violation is still PRINTED — the scope narrows the verdict
# and never the search. The reason is that `verdict=` is the one figure a reader
# acts on, and a violation nobody will repair cannot be acted on: over fusion's
# own repository 297 of 299 rows sat in text nobody edits, so the line was
# pinned at `violations` and carried no information. Archived-ness is NOT the
# criterion — the frozen stores are under a quarter of that mass.
#
# In scope: a live workbench record — a Circle record in any state,
# portfolio.md, an open issue, a decision carrying `_o_` or `_a_`, a plan
# carrying `_o_` or `_p_` — and everything outside the workbench, where no
# marker exists and every file is live (CLAUDE.md, rules/, .claude/rules/,
# docs/ and every declared path). Out of scope: the frozen stores, an issue or
# decision at a terminal marker, a closed or deferred plan, and the kinds that
# carry no marker at all — history, analyses, reviews, consult, memos,
# investigations, which record what was true when they were written. That last
# class is a judgement rather than a derivation and is reasoned in
# hooks/lib/citation-corpus.ts, the one place the predicate is authored: the
# blocking gate hooks/lib/__tests__/workbench-citation-lint.test.ts reads the
# same function as its corpus, so the two cannot drift apart.
#
# `edited-files` says how many of `files` were in that scope, and
# `edited-violations` / `unedited-violations` split the printed rows the same
# way — they sum to dangling + store-prefixed, and the first is what `verdict=`
# reads. A scoped verdict whose scope is not in the output would be worse than
# an unscoped one, so those three figures are part of the block rather than
# extras on it. Each violation row repeats the split in a column between the
# status and the problem, `edited` or `not-edited`. An `--undecidable` row
# carries no such column: it reaches no verdict by kind, before any scoping
# question is asked.
#
# `unrewritable-violations` is how many of the printed rows the grammar has
# itself ruled must stand as written, so that a reader can tell the violations a
# human may repair from the ones nothing is allowed to touch. Such a row is a
# verbatim exhibit — a quoted transcript, a worked example — judged on its shape
# and skipped by the sweep named below, which rewrites no token carrying an
# exemption reason, because respelling an exhibit deletes what it was filed to
# show. It is named for that property rather than for the exemptions producing
# it today, and each violation row repeats it in a column after the scope one,
# `unrewritable` or `rewritable`; `rewritable` says only that no exemption
# forbids respelling that token, not that a rewrite exists for it. The figure is
# a marginal over the same rows as `edited-violations`, not a subset of it, and
# `verdict=` does not read it: what makes the verdict `violations` is still
# `edited-violations` > 0 and nothing else.
#
# Corpus: every .md under fusion-workbench/, the frozen stores read exactly
# like the live tree since commit 32fe0d49 deleted the exclusion, plus
# CLAUDE.md, rules/*.md, .claude/rules/*.md and docs/**/*.md at the project
# root, where present. The grammar is hooks/lib/citation-scan.ts, the same one
# fusion's own gates run; the form it reads is stated in
# rules/fusion-workbench-conventions.md "Filename Patterns".
# `--undecidable` adds one row per bare stamp or ambiguous token, which reach
# no verdict: a bare stamp names a minute, not a file.
#
# Plus the declared corpus: every file the project named in citations.extraPaths
# in its fusion.json, as git pathspecs under :(glob), deduplicated against the
# above by absolute path. That is how a NON-MARKDOWN file enters — outside
# Markdown there is no fence and no blockquote, so nothing in a file's text
# tells a pointer from an exhibit, and the project declares instead. declared-
# patterns= is what the project wrote and declared-files= what those patterns
# name; declared-files reads `unavailable` where git would not answer, which is
# never a 0, because a 0 would report an empty declaration as a taken count.
# A pattern matching nothing, or refused for being absolute or carrying a `..`
# segment, is named on stderr and never on stdout. Declare nothing and the
# corpus is the Markdown above, exactly as it was before the leaf existed.
#
# bin/fusion-citation-sweep resolves the same leaf through the same function:
# the two hand-run helpers share one corpus, because a reporter narrower than
# the rewriter is how the sweep came to change files this check declared clean.
# The blocking gate hooks/lib/__tests__/workbench-citation-lint.test.ts does
# NOT read the declaration, on purpose: it runs in everyone's npm test with no
# approvable baseline, so a corpus set by an editable configuration leaf would
# redden the suite of somebody who edited nothing.
#
# It decides nothing per line about pointer versus statement — a fenced code
# block or a blockquote exempts a token, and that is the whole distinction.
# It never rewrites: the rewriter is bin/fusion-citation-sweep, run by hand
# after reading its --dry-run census and behind its own three guards.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  the check ran. `verdict=` says what it found. A violation is NOT a
#      non-zero exit, deliberately — same reason bin/fusion-review-coverage and
#      bin/fusion-staging-drift give (issue 260810-0710_*_the-drift-checks-last-line-makes-the-whole-block-exit-non-zero-when-no-circle-is-active.md).
#   1  usage error.
#   2  no fusion workbench above the working directory; nothing to check.
#   3  the plugin's compiled hooks are missing, so nothing could be asked. Its
#      own code and not 2: "there is nothing to check" is an answer about the
#      project, and a broken install must never be reported as one.
#
# The work is hooks/dist/citation-check.js, resolved relative to this script
# rather than through $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, so an install copy and a work tree
# each run their own build. A call site guards with [ -x ]: the installed copy
# need not carry a helper added between releases (decision 260810-0921_*_how-should-a-prompt-call-a-bin-helper-that-the-installed-copy-may-not-have.md).

set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
entry="$here/../hooks/dist/citation-check.js"

if [ ! -f "$entry" ]; then
  echo "fusion-citation-check: $entry is missing — the plugin's compiled hooks are not installed." >&2
  exit 3
fi

exec node "$entry" "$@"
